'''zebra-parrakeet''' (zē′brä̤-par″a̤-kēt), ''n.'' A kind of grass-parrakeet, ''Mélopsittacus undulatus'', much of whose plumage is barred. It is a common cage-bird. See cut under ''Melopsittacus''.

'''Zebrapicus''' (zē-bra̤-pī′kus), ''n.'' [NL. (Malherbe, 1849), also ''Zebrípicus'' (Bonaparte, 1854), < ''zebra'', q. v., + NL. ''Picus''.] A genus of woodpeckers: so called from the extensive striping of the plumage. {{smaller|It has covered a number of American forms, but was based on the common red-bellied woodpecker of the United States, and is thus a synonym of ''Centurus'' (itself often merged in ''Melanerpes''). See cut under ''Centurus''.}}

'''zebra-plant''' (zē′brä̤-plant), ''n.'' A striped-leafed plant, ''Maranta zebrina''. See ''Maranta''.

'''zebra-poison''' (zē′brä̤-poi″zn), ''n.'' A succulent tree, ''Euphorbia arbórea'', of South Africa. {{smaller|The milky juice is so poisonous as to kill zebras which drink water in which the branches have been placed, and it is sometimes used as an arrow-poison. ''J. Smith'', Dict. of Economic Plants.}}

